<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1810–1862</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daeng Ibrahim was the temenggong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> from 1841 to 1862 and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenggong-abdul-rahman">Temenggong Abdul Rahman</a>. Enriched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>'s trade and by gambier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> planting by Chinese settlers in Johor, he became the real power in the state, and in 1855 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ali ceded him the government of Johor in a treaty arranged by the British. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-bahru">Tanjung Puteri</a>, later Johor Bahru, and was the father of Abu Bakar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temenggong-daeng-ibrahim.jpg" alt="Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim" width="210" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temenggong_Daeng_Ibrahim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
